/* Twelfth editorial pass B: Greg scene depth and original dialogue. */
(function(){"use strict";const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const revisions={"stars-between-the-strings":{"su-23":{"number":23,"title":"The Price of Being Seen","word_count":1195,"content":"The newspaper photograph caught Greg in the middle of laughing and me in the middle of looking at him.\n\nIt should have been sweet. Instead the caption described me as the person who had finally settled Beach City's most eligible wandering musician, which was a remarkable number of assumptions to pack into fourteen words.\n\n\"Most eligible?\" Greg said. \"I wish somebody had told me.\"\n\n\"That's the part you're worried about?\"\n\n\"No. That's the part I can joke about without sweating through my shirt.\"\n\nWe stood beside the laundromat's humming washers, reading the little local paper above a rack of detergent. The article was about a neighborhood music night. We'd helped set up microphones and stayed late to stack chairs. Somehow the reporter had decided the important story was my relationship status.\n\nA customer behind us wanted to know whether the next concert would be free. Greg answered her politely and promised to check the community schedule. Then we took the paper to the editor's office two doors down.\n\nShe looked exhausted. Ink marked the side of her thumb; a stack of late notices had eaten half her desk.\n\n\"I'm glad you liked the picture,\" she began.\n\n\"We didn't come about the picture,\" I said.\n\nHer smile faltered. \"The caption?\"\n\n\"It says I settled him. I didn't. We chose to spend time together. And there are other people in my life who didn't agree to become part of this story.\"\n\nThe editor's eyes went to Greg.\n\nHe raised both hands. \"I think Jasper explained it pretty well.\"\n\nShe tapped her pen. \"People are interested. Greg's been around forever. It's a local feel-good piece.\"\n\n\"Then let it feel good without claiming we signed an ownership deed,\" I said.\n\nI heard how sharp I sounded and took a breath. \"I'm ethically non-monogamous. I prefer polyamory. Greg knows, and we make our agreements privately. I don't want a headline that pretends he rescued me from every other relationship, or that I'm his reward for being lovable.\"\n\nGreg looked at the floor and smiled at his shoes.\n\nThe editor read the caption again.\n\n\"You're right,\" she said. \"I was trying to make a story out of a shortcut.\"\n\nGreg cleared his throat. \"Could you maybe mention the two teenagers who kept the sound board from falling over? They did the actual heroics.\"\n\n\"Not without asking them and their parents,\" the editor said.\n\n\"Good point.\"\n\nWe settled on a correction that named the music night, the performers who had agreed to be credited, and the money raised for instrument repairs. The next issue could carry the correct information. Online or not, the printed copies already out in town weren't going to vanish.\n\nThe editor called us back before we reached the door.\n\n\"One more question,\" she said. \"If the caption was wrong, how should I describe your relationship when I cover the next concert?\"\n\n\"By describing the concert,\" I said.\n\nShe looked down at her notebook, then back at me. \"I suppose that sounds obvious.\"\n\n\"It does now.\"\n\nGreg shifted beside me. He wasn't angry with her, exactly. He'd spent enough years hoping a newspaper would say his name to know how hard it was to resist an easy story.\n\n\"I don't want to pretend I'm single just because it's a shorter caption,\" he said. \"But I don't want a headline about Jasper dating other people either.\"\n\nI felt a rush of relief. We'd agreed to be honest about my ethical non-monogamy and preference for polyamory. That wasn't the same as inviting strangers into our calendar.\n\nThe editor nodded. \"I can write that you performed together without labeling your lives.\"\n\n\"And please don't quote us discussing this,\" I added. \"We're telling you what needs correcting, not giving a new interview.\"\n\nShe drew a line through her notes. I watched her do it. It wasn't a promise that nobody in Beach City would gossip. It was a small correction made by someone who could actually change a printed sentence.\n\nOutside, the woman who'd asked about the free concert was waiting with a flyer. She wanted to know whether the music night had raised enough to repair the children's instruments.\n\n\"Not yet,\" Greg told her. \"That's why we're having another one.\"\n\nShe asked for a contact so she could donate without buying a ticket. He gave her the community center's number, not ours. She left before either of us could turn her generosity into a story about ourselves.\n\nI leaned against the van.\n\n\"That was exhausting.\"\n\n\"Yeah. Want me to drive somewhere with no reporters?\"\n\n\"Anywhere with sandwiches.\"\n\n\"That's a beautiful vision of the future.\"\n\nHe reached out, paused, and waited for me to take his hand. I did. It wasn't the newspaper's version of a settled musician and a person who'd rescued him from wandering. It was a man with ink on his thumb and a person who still had other people to love, making a choice for one ordinary afternoon.\n\nWe could tell that story ourselves, or not tell it at all.\n\nOutside, Greg leaned against the van.\n\n\"You okay?\" he asked.\n\n\"Embarrassed. Annoyed. Weirdly hungry.\"\n\n\"That's a whole album.\"\n\n\"I'd buy it.\"\n\nHe nudged my shoulder with his. \"You know, I liked how you said we chose each other.\"\n\n\"It was true.\"\n\n\"I got scared you were going to be mad at me for not stepping in sooner.\"\n\n\"You let me speak for myself. That wasn't abandoning me.\"\n\n\"Good. Because I was also busy thinking of twelve terrible jokes.\"\n\n\"Only twelve?\"\n\n\"I've got standards.\"\n\nWe got sandwiches from the boardwalk and carried them to the van. Someone had taped a flyer to its window asking Greg to perform at a fundraiser he hadn't heard about. He read it, sighed, and wrote down the contact number. Being liked in a small town meant people assumed you could always say yes.\n\n\"You can decline,\" I said.\n\n\"Yeah. I know. I'm trying to remember that.\"\n\nA gull stole a corner of my sandwich. Greg laughed so hard he nearly lost his drink. I chased the bird a grand total of three steps before realizing the humiliation was complete.\n\nLater, at the car wash office, we drafted a response to the fundraising committee and made a list of gigs he actually wanted to play. We kept separate calendars. He asked which dates affected us; I told him the ones that did. Neither of us needed a report on every other person I saw.\n\nThe correction came out the following week. It was small, under the community notices. It gave credit to the musicians and volunteers. It didn't put me on display.\n\nGreg read it twice.\n\n\"They spelled my name right,\" he said. \"That's new.\"\n\nHe reached for my hand. I let him take it.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe kissed beside the van where anyone might have seen, and then I drew back laughing at his hair getting caught in the open window.\n\n\"You all right?\" I asked.\n\n\"Romantically compromised,\" he said.\n\nI shut the window. We kept the rest of the evening for ourselves, not because there was anything shameful to hide, but because some things were ours to choose."},"su-26":{"number":26,"title":"Second Verse","word_count":1283,"content":"Greg had written a second verse on the back of a receipt.\n\nThe first verse filled nearly a page of his notebook. The second consisted of three crossed-out words, a coffee stain, and a drawing of what might have been a sad guitar. I studied it while he adjusted the recording levels.\n\n\"Powerful,\" I said.\n\n\"It's a work in progress.\"\n\n\"The sad guitar moved me.\"\n\n\"That's a self-portrait.\"\n\nThe garage was crowded with equipment, half the cables labeled and the other half apparently living by instinct. A blue light on the cassette recorder blinked at us. Outside, a delivery truck went past, rattling the window hard enough to shake a loose pencil off the shelf.\n\nWe'd agreed to spend the afternoon working on something new. I hadn't understood how difficult new could be for somebody whose older songs still carried so much of his life.\n\nGreg tested a chord, stopped, and started it again.\n\n\"Does it sound too much like something I used to play?\" he asked.\n\n\"Which something?\"\n\nHe didn't answer right away.\n\nI sat on the floor beside the mixing desk. \"You can say Rose's name.\"\n\n\"I know. Sometimes I worry that if I bring her into a room, you won't have enough space left in it.\"\n\n\"That's a pretty crowded room you've imagined.\"\n\nHe rubbed the back of his neck. \"Yeah.\"\n\nI reached for the notebook, then stopped. \"Can I write an idea down?\"\n\n\"Please. Save me from the guitar face.\"\n\nI wrote a sentence about a road that changed under the wheels without pretending the places behind us hadn't been real. It wasn't a lyric yet. It was something we could disagree about.\n\nGreg read it. \"That feels too neat.\"\n\n\"How?\"\n\n\"Roads don't always take you someplace better. Sometimes you just take a different turn.\"\n\n\"Then write that.\"\n\nHe picked up the pencil. Half a minute later he was laughing.\n\n\"Great. I made it depressing.\"\n\n\"You made it true.\"\n\nWe worked for an hour and produced a spectacularly bad bridge, which wasn't a good sign for a song or the people singing it. Greg played one version with so much enthusiasm that a loose speaker vibrated off its stand. I caught the cable before it dragged the recorder with it.\n\n\"That's it,\" I said. \"The equipment is voting no.\"\n\nGreg looked mournfully at the stand. \"Critics.\"\n\nWe took a break to repair it. He showed me how to wrap a cable properly rather than letting me pretend I knew. I was grateful he didn't turn the lesson into a demonstration of how talented he was; he made mistakes, too, and admitted when he'd mislabeled a channel.\n\nThe repaired stand held while Greg tuned the guitar again. I wanted to applaud the equipment simply for staying where it belonged.\n\n\"You're treating that cable like it's personally betrayed you,\" I said.\n\n\"It knows what it did.\"\n\nHe pulled the recorder's old label off and replaced it with one showing the channel numbers. The garage had a thousand tiny signs of being used by somebody who'd once had to carry everything alone: a spare string tucked under a mug, tools in the guitar case, a good microphone wrapped in an old shirt. Being able to afford better equipment hadn't taught him how to stop bracing for it to break.\n\nI asked whether Steven might want to hear the new song when it was ready.\n\n\"Maybe,\" Greg said. \"I don't want him to think I'm trying to write something about his mom without asking.\"\n\n\"Then don't say it's about her unless you mean it.\"\n\nGreg looked at the receipt in his hand. \"The first verse came from me thinking about someone who isn't here. The second keeps trying to turn into a promise to somebody who's right in front of me.\"\n\n\"Do you think a promise has to be the end of the song?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\nI felt a little foolish admitting that I didn't either. We had a calendar with several people's names on it, different plans in different places, and an agreement that affection wasn't a contest. None of that supplied a rhyme.\n\n\"Could the next verse be about what you do when you're afraid?\" I asked.\n\n\"Probably a terrible song. Mostly I check the tires on the van.\"\n\n\"Then maybe that's where it starts.\"\n\nHe laughed. Then he wrote down *check the tires* in the notebook, shook his head, and crossed it out. A moment later he put it back.\n\nWe found a rhythm in the sound of the laundry dryer next door, a small uneven thump that landed just after the beat. Greg tried to copy it on the guitar and ended up inventing a melody neither of us recognized.\n\n\"Do you like that?\" I asked.\n\nHe played it again.\n\n\"I do. Even if nobody else ever hears it.\"\n\nThat seemed to frighten him more than the possibility of a bad review. He had spent years practicing for an audience, even when he said he didn't care.\n\nI sat on the edge of the workbench, close but not close enough to touch him by accident. He put the guitar down for a moment and reached for my hand.\n\n\"Thanks for letting a song be just a song,\" he said.\n\n\"You're welcome. Although I reserve the right to judge the sad-guitar drawing.\"\n\n\"It's my best portrait.\"\n\nWe laughed so hard we had to start the recording again.\n\nAs he retightened a knob, he said, \"I used to think the way to prove I meant something was to turn every feeling into a song.\"\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I'm trying to work out whether I can just tell you stuff.\"\n\n\"You can.\"\n\n\"Okay.\" He set the screwdriver down. \"I miss her. Sometimes I miss the version of me who didn't know how complicated everything would get. And I really, really like being here with you.\"\n\nI felt my chest tighten.\n\n\"Thank you for telling me.\"\n\n\"That's it?\"\n\n\"Did you want a drum solo?\"\n\nHe laughed. Then he looked at me with genuine uncertainty.\n\nI moved closer. \"I've told you I prefer polyamory, that I'm ethically non-monogamous. I love different people in different ways. It doesn't mean I want each relationship to prove it can replace the others. I don't want you to erase Rose to make room for me. And I don't need to erase the people I care about when I'm with you.\"\n\nHe took a breath. \"So I can write a song that isn't a competition.\"\n\n\"Please. Competition songs sound exhausting.\"\n\nWe went back to the second verse. It was rougher now, less clever. He played it once, stopped, and revised two notes. When he let me hear the full song again, the new part didn't explain the old one away. It let the melody move forward.\n\nWe had to finish packing the instruments before dinner. Someone was borrowing the room the next morning, and there was a real schedule to honor. I labeled the last cable and watched Greg put the notebook into the case instead of leaving it where the damp could curl its pages.\n\nAt the door, he stopped.\n\n\"Could I kiss you before we leave?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe kiss was a little clumsy because his guitar case was wedged between us. He put it down. I pulled him close, laughing when he nearly tripped over the strap. For a moment the garage smelled only of dust, warm electronics, and him.\n\nHe shut off the recording light.\n\nWe didn't finish the arrangement that night. We did finish talking. When I came back the following week, the notebook was still open to the second verse, and Greg had penciled one extra word in the margin: *again*."},"su-30":{"number":30,"title":"The Brightest Room","word_count":1204,"content":"Greg volunteered both of us to repaint the community center.\n\nHe did it in front of six people and a woman holding a clipboard, which meant I couldn't even throw a paintbrush at him without witnesses.\n\n\"Both?\" I said.\n\nHe glanced sideways. \"I meant to ask.\"\n\n\"Before or after you gave away my Saturday?\"\n\n\"Somewhere in the middle.\"\n\nThe building had been dark long enough that the windows wore a film of dust. A section of the floor was taped off where the boards had lifted. There were chairs stacked in the hallway and old music stands leaning against a wall. Nobody knew whether the heating bill could be covered all winter.\n\nThe clipboard woman, who introduced herself as Pilar, handed us the budget. Volunteers had done what they could. A local donor offered to pay for new lights, but he wanted approval over the program and a say in who was allowed to perform.\n\n\"Sounds like a pretty big gift,\" Greg said.\n\n\"It's a purchase,\" I answered.\n\nPilar looked relieved that somebody had said it out loud.\n\nAt the next meeting, the donor arrived in a clean coat and spoke about restoring pride to the neighborhood. He wasn't unkind. He just wanted to put conditions on every dollar. Youth music nights could continue, he said, as long as performances stayed appropriate and the committee signed off on each artist.\n\n\"Who decides appropriate?\" I asked.\n\n\"The board.\"\n\n\"Who appoints the board?\"\n\n\"I would have a seat.\"\n\nGreg leaned back. He usually rescued awkward conversations with a joke, but this time he didn't.\n\n\"I was thirteen when somebody told me my music wasn't suitable for a serious stage,\" he said. \"They weren't wrong that it was loud. They were wrong that it meant I didn't deserve to play.\"\n\nThe donor folded his hands. \"You need funding.\"\n\n\"We do,\" Pilar said. \"But we need a community center even more.\"\n\nThe meeting ended without a donation. The lights stayed old for another week, and I spent most of the evening wondering whether we had done the right thing.\n\nGreg found me scrubbing paint from my fingernails.\n\n\"You think we blew it?\" he asked.\n\n\"I think I wanted money and didn't want the strings. I didn't like being made to choose.\"\n\n\"Me either.\"\n\nWe worked out a smaller plan with Pilar. Repair the safe rooms first. Open one evening a week. Keep the floor section closed until a qualified inspection. Publish the actual costs on the notice board, including the cost of paying the people doing skilled work. Greg offered a free performance but was careful not to promise somebody else's labor. I took charge of the volunteer schedule after asking each person what they could do.\n\nPilar's smaller plan came with a new problem: three people wanted the same practice room on the first evening, and all three had reasons that mattered.\n\nOne was a teenager saving for their own instrument. One was an older woman who had nowhere else to practice a violin without disturbing the neighbors. The third was a group whose drummer worked nights and could only come at a very particular hour.\n\nGreg looked at the schedule and rubbed his forehead. \"I thought the hard part was getting the lights on.\"\n\n\"That was a hard part,\" Pilar said. \"This one's another hard part.\"\n\nWe asked the musicians what time they actually needed instead of moving colored boxes around until the paper looked tidy. The violinist was happy to start early. The teenagers asked for a session where nobody watched them practice. The drummer could make the late slot if the room closed on time.\n\nI wrote the agreement on the board. Greg moved the amplifier away from the doorway after Pilar pointed out the cable was crossing the entrance. We borrowed a cord cover from the car wash, returned it at the end of the night and recorded the loan rather than leaving a mystery for the next volunteer.\n\nOne donor came by to see why we hadn't installed the expensive lights he'd offered.\n\n\"People will come if this place looks impressive,\" he said.\n\nPilar looked around at the ordinary bulbs, the patched chairs, and the small group of children tuning their instruments.\n\n\"People already came,\" she said.\n\n\"You're not thinking big enough.\"\n\n\"Maybe,\" she answered. \"But they can use this room tonight.\"\n\nHe left without agreeing to give money. Greg watched him go, then glanced at me.\n\n\"Do we have to feel noble about being broke?\"\n\n\"No. We can be annoyed. Then we can check whether the roof needs repairing.\"\n\nThe roof did need repairing. We added it to the list before ordering any more paint.\n\nLater a parent asked whether the teenagers could perform at the next open night. Greg crouched to their height and asked them, not their parent, whether they wanted an audience.\n\n\"Maybe later,\" one said.\n\n\"Cool,\" Greg replied. \"You can just learn a song first.\"\n\nI watched their shoulders relax.\n\nThere was nothing magical about the building. It needed wiring, money, honest staff hours and a room where saying no wouldn't mean losing your place. For the first time, I could imagine it lasting.\n\nOn the first night, three people showed up to play and two just came to listen. A teenager brought a badly tuned instrument, looking as though someone might laugh. Greg tuned it with them rather than taking it away and playing a dazzling demonstration.\n\nPilar rearranged the chairs when she noticed a narrow turning space beside the door. A volunteer moved the refreshment table out of the route. We didn't call it a heroic act. We just made the room usable.\n\nNear the end of the night, a gust shook the windows and the lights blinked. Everyone froze.\n\n\"Old wiring,\" Greg said.\n\n\"Old wiring we need checked,\" I corrected.\n\nPilar added it to the list. We closed the back room until the electrician could inspect it. The concert moved to the safer side of the building, where the sound was smaller and the audience could still hear every word.\n\nAfter the last visitor left, Greg found me standing in the glow from the streetlamps.\n\n\"Hey,\" he said. \"About Saturday.\"\n\n\"Yes?\"\n\n\"I shouldn't have signed you up.\"\n\n\"No, you shouldn't.\"\n\nHe rubbed the back of his neck. \"I figured you'd want to help.\"\n\n\"I did want to help. I also wanted you to ask.\"\n\nHe nodded. \"Next time, I ask.\"\n\n\"We can put that on the clipboard.\"\n\n\"Is there a punishment for repeat offenders?\"\n\n\"Sorting the tangled extension cords.\"\n\nHe winced. \"Cruel and unusual.\"\n\nWe laughed. He kissed my cheek, paused, and waited. I turned toward him and kissed him properly. Paint dried on my sleeve; the room still needed a lot of work. Outside, Beach City carried on being itself.\n\nI wasn't looking for a perfect building. I was looking for somewhere people could return without having to earn their place by pretending.\n\nThe following week, Pilar posted a revised schedule with a second volunteer lead. My name was on it for the nights I'd actually chosen. Greg's was on the music nights he could make. We each had lives outside the center, and that was part of what made the work sustainable."},"su-32":{"number":32,"title":"The Work of Staying","word_count":1234,"content":"The application had been sitting under the car wash receipts for two days.\n\nGreg wasn't snooping. He was looking for the electricity bill, which was somehow more embarrassing. He lifted the envelope, saw my name, and put it back on the table without opening it.\n\n\"That looks important,\" he said.\n\n\"It is.\"\n\n\"Want to tell me?\"\n\nI looked at the plastic chair across from him. I wanted to say it wasn't decided, which was true, and leave the rest until I knew what I wanted. But he would notice me being careful with every word.\n\n\"I got offered a job,\" I said. \"Not here.\"\n\nHe smiled immediately. \"That's fantastic.\"\n\n\"It's across the water. I'd have to spend longer stretches away from Beach City.\"\n\n\"Still fantastic.\"\n\nThe fluorescent light buzzed above us. I watched him straighten a stack of receipts that didn't need straightening.\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"You can be happy for me and still have feelings about the distance.\"\n\nHe stopped moving. \"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why are you pretending this is as easy as finding a new parking spot?\"\n\nHe looked down. \"Because I was afraid if I said I'd miss you, it'd sound like I was asking you to give it up.\"\n\nThere it was. The thing beneath the joke. I pulled out the other chair.\n\n\"Sit?\"\n\nHe sat. The electricity bill fell from his hand and landed upside down between us.\n\n\"I'm not asking permission,\" I said. \"I am asking whether you want to talk about what this would mean for us.\"\n\n\"Yeah. I do.\"\n\nWe started with the stupidly practical parts because they were the parts we could actually do something about. The job paid enough to cover travel if I didn't spend it all getting back and forth for one-night visits. Greg had bookings on certain weekends, including a school fundraiser he'd already promised to play. I had other partners and commitments beyond Beach City; none of those disappeared because a job offer existed.\n\n\"I know you've been clear about being ethically non-monogamous,\" he said. \"And that you prefer polyamory. I guess I keep thinking a schedule's gonna make me sound like I'm booking a slot in your life.\"\n\n\"You are in my life. A schedule just means I don't promise to be in two places on the same Thursday.\"\n\nHe laughed softly. \"That'd make a good song.\"\n\n\"Only if you leave the receipts out of it.\"\n\nWe wrote down what we could promise. We would tell each other about changes early enough to matter. Neither of us would treat silence as a punishment. We could say we wanted more time without using guilt to get it. Visits would be invitations, not surprise tests. If either of us couldn't make something work, we would talk about it instead of announcing a failure.\n\nWhen we finished the first page of our plan, I went to the board by the sink and wrote down the dates I'd have to answer the job offer. I needed an actual deadline, not one I could move whenever the decision made me nervous.\n\nGreg looked at it. \"So there's an interview?\"\n\n\"Yes. A second one. I didn't tell you because I didn't know whether I wanted to go.\"\n\n\"Do you?\"\n\n\"I want to find out what the work is like when nobody's trying to impress me.\"\n\nHe thought about that, then nodded.\n\nWe made a list of questions I could ask without surrendering the chance to accept. Would the job pay for travel when I needed to cross the water? Who approved a schedule change? Would I be expected to take on duties that belonged to people already working there? If I chose to leave after the trial period, would I still be able to come back without being treated as a traitor?\n\nGreg laughed at the last one. \"That sounds like a lot of your life.\"\n\n\"It is.\"\n\nHe stood, opened the little office window, and let in air that smelled of soap and summer asphalt. The car wash machinery had stopped for the day. The silence made the room feel like someplace we could choose to be.\n\n\"Can I tell you what scares me?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Not that you'll like the job. I hope you do. I think I'm scared I'll start treating your coming home like proof you still love me, and then every delayed bus will make me a jerk.\"\n\nI leaned against the desk. \"Then we have to notice when that starts happening. I can't guarantee I won't miss a visit. I can guarantee I won't make you guess whether I mean to come.\"\n\nHe looked toward the calendar.\n\n\"Could we plan the first week after the interview before either of us promises the whole year?\"\n\n\"That sounds perfect.\"\n\n\"Wow. You said perfect. Write that down.\"\n\nI did, in the margin of the page he was holding. He tried not to smile and failed.\n\nOur non-exclusive relationship wasn't a test of how much distance we could endure. It was two people trying not to let the fear of a future decision take the place of one we could make today.\n\nI asked whether he wanted to walk to the diner before we returned to the application. He did. He held out his hand, and when I took it he looked as pleased as he had the first time we played a song together.\n\nGreg pointed at the page. \"This feels very grown-up.\"\n\n\"Terrifying, isn't it?\"\n\n\"I was hoping love came with fewer columns.\"\n\nI nudged his knee with mine. \"You have a business. You know columns.\"\n\n\"That's different. The columns at the car wash don't have feelings.\"\n\n\"Your accounts would suggest otherwise.\"\n\nHe laughed, then went quiet.\n\n\"I do want you to stay,\" he said. \"I know I don't get to decide for you. But I want you to know that.\"\n\nThe admission made my throat tighten. There was something braver about hearing his preference without having to fight for my freedom first.\n\n\"Thank you,\" I said. \"I haven't decided.\"\n\n\"Okay.\"\n\n\"And if I take the job, I still want to come home here sometimes. Not because I owe you proof. Because I like this stupid office and your terrible coffee.\"\n\n\"Hey. The coffee is improving.\"\n\n\"It's learning to apologize.\"\n\nHe reached across the table. \"Can I hold your hand?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe sat with our fingers tangled between the bill and the job application. He didn't ask to see the letter. I didn't hand it over to prove I trusted him.\n\nAfter closing, we walked to the van. The air had turned cool. A light flickered in the car wash sign, and he made a note to call someone about it before the next wet morning.\n\nInside the van, he put on a quiet recording—not one of the old songs that felt too loaded, but a rough instrumental he'd made the previous week. I leaned against his shoulder.\n\n\"Stay for dinner?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes. Tonight.\"\n\n\"Tonight's pretty good.\"\n\nHe kissed me when I turned toward him. We had all the time we wanted for that moment, and no right to assume anything about the next one. Later, we went inside and let the night become ours.\n\nIn the morning, the application was still unanswered. I put it in the drawer beside my toothbrush. We would talk again when I knew more, not pretend a single night could settle a whole future."},"su-37":{"number":37,"title":"The Quiet Encore","word_count":1424,"content":"The last audience member had gone home, but Greg was still working.\n\nHe was coiling a cable when I found him. The Starwake stage stood empty, its lanterns dark except for the safety light near the steps. Someone had left a paper cup under the bench. The wind made it roll in small circles.\n\n\"Are we done?\" I asked.\n\n\"Almost.\"\n\n\"That's what you said twenty minutes ago.\"\n\n\"Time is a mysterious concept to musicians.\"\n\nHe grinned without looking up.\n\nGreg had agreed to play one set at the Listening Fair. He played beautifully. Then he'd helped carry chairs, checked the microphone, found missing keys and offered to stay until every piece of equipment was packed.\n\nThe actual cleanup volunteers were standing nearby, waiting for him to hand over the last two cables.\n\nI touched his elbow.\n\n\"Greg, they're here.\"\n\nHe looked at the workers.\n\n\"Oh. Right.\"\n\nNola, the shift leader, held out her hand.\n\n\"We've got the equipment from here. That was the agreement.\"\n\n\"Sure, but I can help with—\"\n\n\"Your shift ended.\"\n\nHe stopped and gave her the cable. She smiled.\n\n\"Thanks for the music. Go rest.\"\n\nWe walked to the safe bench along the pier. The western boards had passed inspection with posted limits. The strange low note beneath the supports hadn't sounded that evening; Fen's tide chart showed an ordinary tide. Beyond the lanterns, the sea moved in dark folds.\n\nGreg sat and stared toward the empty stage.\n\n\"You were quiet during the last song,\" he said.\n\n\"I liked it.\"\n\n\"Was it too slow?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Too sad?\"\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Are you asking whether I liked the music, or whether I liked you?\"\n\nHe laughed weakly.\n\n\"Probably both.\"\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n\"I liked the music. I love you. I'm allowed to be quiet while I listen.\"\n\nHe smiled, then looked embarrassed.\n\n\"Everybody goes home when the music stops,\" he said. \"I used to think if I kept the lights on long enough, no one would forget me.\"\n\n\"Is that why you keep doing everyone else's cleanup shifts?\"\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\n\"Does it help?\"\n\nHe looked at his hands.\n\n\"No. Mostly it makes me tired and a little resentful nobody knows I'm tired.\"\n\n\"Have you told anyone?\"\n\n\"I'm telling you.\"\n\nI reached toward him, then paused.\n\n\"Want me close?\"\n\n\"Yeah.\"\n\nI moved closer. He put an arm around my shoulders, and we shared the blanket he'd brought for me.\n\n\"Did you bring one for yourself?\" I asked.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then this one is ours for a while.\"\n\nHe tucked it around both of us.\n\nGreg was generous with things he could offer without being asked. It took much more courage for him to admit he needed something. I recognized the habit. I had spent too many years hoping people would notice what I couldn't bring myself to say.\n\n\"Can I ask you something difficult?\" he said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Do you ever worry I'm only happy when you need me?\"\n\n\"Sometimes I worry you use being useful to avoid saying what you want.\"\n\n\"That's probably fair.\"\n\n\"What do you want tonight?\"\n\nHe opened his mouth, then shut it.\n\n\"Not another concert.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Not a big talk about forever.\"\n\n\"Also good.\"\n\nHe looked almost shy.\n\n\"I want to sit here until the maintenance lights go out. Then maybe get something warm to eat. Have one night where neither of us needs to be interesting.\"\n\nMy chest tightened.\n\n\"I want that too.\"\n\n\"Wow. A perfectly matched set of glamorous wishes.\"\n\nWe laughed.\n\nNola finished locking the equipment shed and called a final goodnight. Greg thanked her without offering another shift. She had her own work and her own evening; we didn't have to become part of it.\n\nNola left the spare key on its hook before locking the shed. She had carried half the equipment that night; I wanted to remember that when the story became a romantic memory in my head.\n\n\"She's good at her job,\" Greg said, watching her go.\n\n\"She is. You are too.\"\n\n\"Apparently I'm great at working past the part where somebody told me to go home.\"\n\n\"You're improving.\"\n\nHe sat beside me, then leaned back until his shoulders met the cool railing.\n\n\"There's an encore I still want,\" he said.\n\nI looked at the empty stage. \"Didn't you already do the encore?\"\n\n\"Not that kind.\"\n\nHe paused long enough that I started to worry he'd forgotten what he meant.\n\n\"One night when we don't promise to do anything for anybody,\" he said. \"We could go somewhere cheap for food. I could tell you a bad joke. You could tell me I'm terrible. Then we could go home before somebody asks for a favor.\"\n\n\"That sounds suspiciously like a date.\"\n\n\"Yeah.\"\n\nI had a hundred questions about schedules. I also had a simple answer.\n\n\"I'd like that.\"\n\nHis smile came slowly, with none of the stage brightness he could switch on for strangers. It was the sort of smile that made me want to move closer until the wind stopped feeling cold.\n\nHe noticed, I think, because he reached for my hand and waited. I threaded my fingers through his.\n\n\"I know you don't have to be here every night,\" he said.\n\n\"And I know you don't have to make yourself useful every night.\"\n\n\"Maybe we should make a rule.\"\n\n\"One rule. I'm tired.\"\n\nHe thought about it. \"We can ask for company without inventing a task.\"\n\nI leaned against him. \"Good rule.\"\n\nFor a while we did nothing else. A last boat moved toward its mooring below the pier. Somewhere near the square, the baker's bell rang to say the evening delivery was finished. We could hear people returning home to lives that did not have to include us.\n\nGreg drew the collar of his jacket around my shoulders without insisting I wear it. I pulled it closer and stole a quick kiss.\n\n\"That was a bonus encore,\" he whispered.\n\n\"Don't start selling tickets.\"\n\nHe laughed quietly against my hair.\n\nWe sat until the sea wind made my fingers cold.\n\n\"There's something else,\" Greg said.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"I know you've got other places to go. Other people you love. We talked about ethical non-monogamy and your preference for polyamory when this first became serious. I meant it when I said I wanted to make room.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Sometimes when you leave for another date, I feel lonely. Then I tell myself I shouldn't, because I agreed.\"\n\n\"Loneliness doesn't mean you've broken our agreement,\" I said. \"Neither of us promised never to feel afraid.\"\n\nHe let out a breath.\n\n\"Can I ask for next Sunday?\"\n\n\"Yes. We can plan that. I have Moonroot work in the morning, but the afternoon is free. If it runs late, I'll tell you.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"I'd like that.\"\n\nI rested my head against him. I could love someone without asking them to become the only person I loved. I could also take their loneliness seriously, without treating it as a demand to choose.\n\nOn the walk to the Starwake Door, Greg stopped to point out a tiny fish under the boardwalk, an upside-down lantern reflected in a puddle, and a scrap of paper caught on a nail.\n\n\"You're still working,\" I accused when he bent to pick up the paper.\n\n\"That was one piece of litter.\"\n\n\"An heroic intervention.\"\n\n\"Saved the entire pier.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nAt the late stall, Mara served soup so hot it made Greg burn his tongue. He insisted the temperature proved its artistic intensity. She told him to drink water and stop giving speeches in her doorway.\n\nHe obeyed.\n\nWe split the last roll. He asked if I wanted to go home with him. I said yes because I wanted to, not as a reward for his music.\n\nAt home, we left his guitar in its case. The evening did not need a soundtrack. He kissed me slowly, one hand warm against mine. I caught his sleeve and kissed him again. Later we closed the door and left the rest of the night private.\n\nIn the morning, I found him making breakfast.\n\nNo guitar. No microphone.\n\nJust Greg trying not to burn toast.\n\n\"You look happy,\" I said.\n\n\"I am.\"\n\n\"Without an audience?\"\n\n\"Especially without one.\"\n\nHe handed me a piece of toast. It was almost perfect.\n\nI ate it anyway.\n\nLater, Nola sent a note thanking the volunteers for honoring their scheduled shifts. Greg pinned it beside the old list of shows he thought he had to accept.\n\nThe old list stayed.\n\nThe new note went above it."},"su-45":{"number":45,"title":"A Better Kind of Jealousy","word_count":1475,"content":"The rumor reached Greg before I got back from Brasswatch.\n\nI found him sitting on the hood of the van with a paper cup of coffee and the look he wore when he was trying to decide whether a joke would make something less painful.\n\n\"Hey,\" he said.\n\n\"Hey.\"\n\nHe smiled too quickly.\n\n\"Busy day?\"\n\n\"Very. Danse finally got the workshop door working without a lock you need a ladder to reach.\"\n\n\"That's good.\"\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"What's wrong?\"\n\nHe stared into his coffee.\n\n\"Nothing. Just heard a weird thing.\"\n\nI waited.\n\nHe sighed.\n\n\"Somebody said you're planning to move into Brasswatch permanently. That you and Danse are making some kind of big announcement. That I'm basically the guy you visit when there's music.\"\n\nI blinked.\n\n\"Who said that?\"\n\n\"Does it matter?\"\n\n\"It matters that someone is inventing a story about my life.\"\n\nGreg tried to laugh.\n\n\"Yeah, well, people like a good story.\"\n\n\"That doesn't make it true.\"\n\nHe shrugged, still looking at the cup.\n\nI sat beside him, close enough that our shoulders touched but not so close that he couldn't move away.\n\n\"Do you believe it?\" I asked.\n\n\"No.\" He hesitated. \"Not exactly.\"\n\n\"Then what is it?\"\n\nHe rubbed the heel of his hand against his forehead.\n\n\"I know what we agreed. I know you're ethically non-monogamous, that you prefer polyamory, and that none of this means you're promising to be exclusive with me. I've said yes to being part of your life because I want to be here.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"But sometimes I hear people talk like there's a scoreboard, and I start wondering if I'm losing.\"\n\nThe words were plain enough to make my chest tighten.\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"I'm not proud of that.\"\n\n\"You don't have to be ashamed of feeling insecure.\"\n\n\"I don't want to turn into somebody who needs to know where you are every minute.\"\n\n\"Then don't. Tell me what you're afraid of.\"\n\nHe took a long breath.\n\n\"That one day you'll find somebody whose life fits yours better. Somebody who can build things, fight monsters, fix doors, whatever. And I'll be the guy who makes you laugh until you don't need that anymore.\"\n\nI looked at his hands, rough from strings and work. I thought about the fundraiser, the way he'd learned to say no to a song that hurt him, and the small note he'd left on the dashboard reminding himself not to promise the universe on my behalf.\n\n\"You're not a consolation prize,\" I said.\n\nHe smiled faintly.\n\n\"Wasn't planning to enter a competition.\"\n\n\"Good. Because there isn't one.\"\n\nHe took another breath.\n\n\"I know jealousy doesn't mean somebody's done something wrong.\"\n\n\"Sometimes it tells us we need something. Time. Reassurance. A clearer plan.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Can I ask for a clearer plan?\"\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nWe went inside the little house near the beach and sat at the kitchen table. Greg set the coffee aside and took out a pencil. I brought my notebook.\n\nWe talked about what was actually changing.\n\nI was helping Danse with the workshop and spending more time at Brasswatch during repairs. That didn't mean I was moving there. Iroh's teahouse needed help after the roof leak. Riverstone's bridge working group had a schedule I wanted to respect. I still wanted time with Greg in Beach City and at Starwake Pier.\n\n\"You could have told me the Brasswatch work was taking more hours,\" he said.\n\n\"I thought I had.\"\n\n\"You told me about the door. Not about the whole week.\"\n\nI winced.\n\n\"You're right.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"I'm not asking you to report to me. I just want to know when our plans are likely to change.\"\n\n\"That's fair.\"\n\nI opened my calendar and showed Greg the week he was worried about. I'd marked Brasswatch as a series of half-days because Danse had asked for help reviewing a set of workshop doors. The work had run into a second week when the locking mechanism failed its safety test.\n\n\"That's why I kept talking about the door,\" I said. \"It was the part I thought would be finished.\"\n\nGreg leaned closer, but he didn't reach for the paper until I handed it to him.\n\n\"Did you tell Danse you had plans with me?\"\n\n\"Yes. He knew the days I'd said I was free. I should've told both of you when the work shifted.\"\n\nHe considered that for a moment.\n\n\"Okay. I'm not mad that you spent time with him. I'm mad that I kept finding out about the changes from other people.\"\n\n\"That's fair.\"\n\nI felt embarrassed enough to make a joke and decided not to. The rumor had hurt because it found a real gap in our communication and filled it with the cruelest story available.\n\n\"Would you like to tell me which parts of your life you don't want me to ask about?\" I said.\n\nHe looked puzzled. \"Like what?\"\n\n\"I ask about your shows, your family, when you miss Rose. But maybe there are things you'd rather bring up yourself.\"\n\nGreg rubbed the back of his neck. \"I don't want to hide stuff. I just don't want to feel like every bad mood is an interview.\"\n\n\"Then tell me when you're not ready.\"\n\n\"Will you believe me?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe reached across the seat and held my hand for a moment.\n\n\"Okay. Then I want us to be allowed to miss each other without it becoming evidence that polyamory doesn't work.\"\n\nThat landed hard. We were both entitled to feelings without appointing them judges.\n\n\"I want that too,\" I said.\n\nGreg leaned closer and kissed my cheek. It was so tentative I almost missed the question inside it. I turned toward him.\n\n\"Can I kiss you properly?\"\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nWe kissed slowly until the coffee went cold. When we drew apart, his hair was more untidy than before and I had forgotten what I'd intended to say next.\n\n\"Now,\" he said, \"we can do the terrifying part.\"\n\n\"The calendar?\"\n\n\"The calendar.\"\n\nWe checked the calendar.\n\nGreg wanted a regular evening to play music together, even if I was terrible at it. I wanted one day a month where we could go somewhere without turning the outing into a community service project. He needed the freedom to visit Steven without assuming I'd always come along. I needed him to be able to tell me when gossip hurt instead of pretending it was funny.\n\n\"That last one sounds hard,\" he said.\n\n\"It is.\"\n\n\"I can try.\"\n\n\"That's all I'm asking.\"\n\nHe wrote something in the margin.\n\nI leaned over.\n\n\"What's that?\"\n\n\"Official Greg Rule Number One.\"\n\nI read it.\n\nDON'T LET STRANGERS WRITE YOUR LOVE SONGS.\n\nI laughed.\n\n\"That's a good rule.\"\n\n\"Number Two is probably don't drink three cups of coffee before discussing feelings.\"\n\n\"Also good.\"\n\nHe glanced at me.\n\n\"Can I ask one more thing?\"\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\n\"If you ever do make a big change, will you tell me yourself?\"\n\n\"Yes. Not because you own the decision. Because you matter to me.\"\n\nHe looked relieved.\n\n\"Okay.\"\n\nWe sat for a while without talking. The house smelled of coffee and salt air. A gull called outside, then another answered. Somewhere down the street, a car door slammed.\n\nGreg reached for my hand.\n\n\"I love you,\" he said.\n\n\"I love you too.\"\n\nHe smiled, slow and genuine.\n\nThen he leaned forward, stopped just short of my face, and raised an eyebrow.\n\n\"Am I allowed to kiss you, or do I need to schedule it?\"\n\n\"You can kiss me.\"\n\n\"Good. I was running out of paperwork.\"\n\nHis kiss was warm and a little clumsy. I laughed against his mouth. He pulled back, grinning.\n\n\"There. Much better than coffee.\"\n\n\"Depends on the coffee.\"\n\n\"Rude.\"\n\nI kissed him again.\n\nThe rest of the afternoon belonged to an ordinary day together. We washed the dishes, fixed a loose hinge on a cupboard, and took a walk along the boardwalk. Greg stopped to talk to a musician he knew, and I bought a small packet of sea-salt candy from a shop near the pier.\n\nWe didn't solve jealousy forever.\n\nAt dinner, Greg admitted he still felt a little nervous about the rumor.\n\nI told him he could ask me again if he needed reassurance, and I would answer honestly rather than make him feel foolish.\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"Then I'm going to ask something very important.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Are you staying for the movie?\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Great. Because I've already made popcorn.\"\n\nLater, when the lights were low and the movie had become background noise, he rested his head against my shoulder. I brushed my fingers through his hair, and he took my hand without looking away from the screen.\n\nOutside, the tide moved in and out along Beach City.\n\nIt had always done that.\n\nIt didn't have to choose one shore forever to keep returning."},"su-48":{"number":48,"title":"An Instrument Returned","word_count":1392,"content":"The guitar came back in a cardboard box.\n\nThat was never a good sign.\n\nGreg stood on the steps of Starwake Pier with the box in his hands and an expression that didn't quite know whether to laugh or cry. The instrument belonged to him. He'd lent it to a young musician named Rowan, who had promised to bring it back after the Listening Fair.\n\nRowan stood nearby, twisting their fingers together.\n\n\"I'm really sorry,\" they said.\n\nGreg opened the box.\n\nThe neck was intact, but the bridge had lifted from the body. One string was broken, and a long scratch ran along the lower edge where the finish had been scraped away.\n\nI felt my stomach drop.\n\n\"Oh, Greg.\"\n\nHe looked at the instrument for a long time.\n\nThen he put it down.\n\n\"What happened?\" he asked.\n\nRowan swallowed.\n\n\"The stand tipped when somebody moved a crate. I caught it before it hit the floor, but the case slipped. I thought I could fix the string, and then I saw the bridge.\"\n\n\"Did anyone get hurt?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nGreg breathed out.\n\n\"Okay. That's something.\"\n\nRowan looked startled.\n\n\"You're not angry?\"\n\n\"I'm disappointed. It's an old guitar. I wanted it back in one piece.\"\n\n\"I can pay for the repair.\"\n\n\"Let's find out what it needs before we promise anything.\"\n\nI looked at him. He was upset, and he wasn't hiding it under a joke. That was new enough to make me proud.\n\nWe took the guitar to Brasswatch Open Workshop.\n\nDanse was running a training session on careful measuring. Renn had a row of small metal fittings laid out on the bench. When Greg arrived carrying the box, everyone paused.\n\n\"Musical instrument,\" Danse said.\n\n\"Broken musical instrument,\" Greg corrected.\n\nDanse examined the bridge.\n\n\"The adhesive has failed.\"\n\n\"Yes, but the wood might still be sound,\" Greg said.\n\nDanse reached for a tool.\n\nI touched his sleeve.\n\n\"Ask first.\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\n\"May I examine it?\"\n\nGreg nodded.\n\nThe first ten minutes went badly.\n\nDanse wanted to measure every piece before deciding on a repair. Greg knew which parts of the guitar had always creaked and which changes would affect its sound. Both were right about something. Both kept interrupting the other.\n\n\"If you clamp it there, you'll change the resonance,\" Greg said.\n\n\"If I don't stabilize it, the bridge will lift again.\"\n\n\"I know, but—\"\n\n\"You haven't let me finish.\"\n\n\"Neither have you.\"\n\nI raised a hand.\n\n\"Would either of you like to hear the other person's sentence?\"\n\nThey looked at me.\n\nRenn laughed.\n\n\"That seems fair.\"\n\nWe tried again.\n\nGreg explained how the instrument sounded before the damage and which imperfections were part of its history. Danse described the structural repair needed to make it safe to restring. Renn took notes and asked whether they could handle the small fittings under supervision.\n\nBy afternoon they'd agreed on a plan.\n\nThe bridge could be reattached after the old glue was cleaned away. The damaged finish didn't need to be made invisible. The new string would be matched to the rest of the set. Greg would test the instrument before the repair was considered complete.\n\nRowan arrived after the inspection.\n\n\"How much?\" they asked.\n\nDanse showed them the estimate.\n\nRowan looked relieved.\n\n\"I can afford that if I pay in two parts.\"\n\nGreg nodded.\n\n\"Then let's do that.\"\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n\"Would you still lend me a guitar sometime?\"\n\nGreg looked at the box.\n\n\"Not this one for a while.\"\n\nRowan's face fell.\n\n\"But if you're still learning, I can help you find an instrument you can practice on. One that doesn't cost more than your rent.\"\n\nRowan smiled.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\nI watched Greg carefully. It would have been easy for him to pretend the damage didn't matter because he wanted to be generous. Instead he'd asked for a repair and left room for a relationship that might become different.\n\nBefore the repair began, Rowan returned with a small envelope. They had counted out the first payment and wanted to know whether the rest could be paid after their next shift.\n\nGreg looked at Danse. Danse looked at the estimate.\n\n\"That is what the agreement says,\" Danse answered. \"We don't alter a signed repayment plan because someone seems anxious.\"\n\nRowan relaxed a little. I could see that they'd expected the adults in the room to be angry enough to demand everything at once.\n\n\"Can I help with the repair?\" they asked.\n\n\"Not on this instrument,\" Greg said gently. \"I want the workshop to do it. But you can watch from the marked area, if Danse says it's safe.\"\n\nDanse set out a clean tray, explained the hot tools and the safe path to the exit. He did not ask Rowan to handle expensive equipment in exchange for the privilege of being there. Renn took the scheduled shop shift; everyone whose time was being used would be paid according to their agreement.\n\nThe repair took longer than Greg wanted. He kept hovering in the doorway, then walking away, then returning with a new question he probably knew the answer to already.\n\nI found him by the water pump.\n\n\"You can be upset,\" I said.\n\n\"I know. I don't want to yell at the kid.\"\n\n\"Being upset doesn't mean you're going to.\"\n\nHe looked at his hands. \"That guitar was with me when I didn't have much else.\"\n\nI remembered the storage room, the old posters and the letter he had chosen not to destroy. The scratch on a piece of wood wasn't the most painful part of the repair; it was having to trust somebody else with a thing that had once helped him survive.\n\n\"Do you need me to stay here?\" I asked.\n\n\"If you've got time.\"\n\n\"I do. I can sit with you, or I can help Renn sort the inventory. Your choice.\"\n\nHe smiled, half embarrassed. \"Could you just sit?\"\n\nI sat beside him on the low wall. We watched the workshop door open and close while people came through with their own ordinary problems. He didn't have to turn the mistake into a forgiveness speech. Rowan didn't have to become his protégé to make the repayment acceptable.\n\nWhen the first test note sounded from inside, Greg stood so quickly I nearly lost my balance.\n\nDanse appeared holding the guitar.\n\n\"Not finished,\" he said. \"But the joint is holding.\"\n\nGreg let out a breath, laughed at himself, then reached for my hand. I squeezed back.\n\nAfter Rowan left, Danse began cleaning the old glue from the bridge. Greg watched with folded arms.\n\n\"You're hovering,\" Danse said.\n\n\"That's my guitar.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"An important guitar.\"\n\n\"I understand.\"\n\nGreg looked surprised.\n\nDanse set the tool down.\n\n\"I once owned equipment that held my whole sense of identity. I know it can be difficult to let someone else repair it.\"\n\nGreg smiled.\n\n\"Yeah. Something like that.\"\n\nThey worked together until evening.\n\nWhen the bridge had set enough for the next stage, Danse placed the guitar in a safe rack and labeled it with the date. Greg signed the work order and left his contact information for the final test.\n\nOutside, the sky was turning pink.\n\n\"How do you feel?\" I asked.\n\nGreg looked at the workshop door.\n\n\"Still disappointed.\"\n\n\"That's allowed.\"\n\n\"And a little better.\"\n\n\"Also allowed.\"\n\nHe took my hand.\n\n\"I used to think if somebody broke something I loved, I had to pretend it was fine or they'd think I didn't love them.\"\n\n\"Now?\"\n\n\"Now I think I can tell them it hurt without turning them into a villain.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe leaned close and kissed me, warm and familiar. The smell of salt and wood glue clung to his jacket.\n\n\"Want to get dinner?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Somewhere with chairs that aren't broken?\"\n\n\"That seems ambitious.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nTwo days later, we returned for the sound test. The guitar played clearly, though the repaired bridge made one low note sound slightly warmer than before.\n\nGreg listened.\n\nThen he played the same little melody again.\n\n\"Huh.\"\n\n\"Is that bad?\" Danse asked.\n\nGreg smiled.\n\n\"No. It's different.\"\n\nHe looked at the scratch along the lower edge.\n\n\"I think I'll leave that part.\"\n\nDanse nodded.\n\n\"Your decision.\"\n\nGreg tucked the guitar into its case.\n\n\"Thanks.\"\n\nThey shook hands.\n\nFor once, neither man seemed to need to prove he had been the most useful person in the room."},"su-49":{"number":49,"title":"The Note Below the Water","word_count":1245,"content":"The empty stage made a sound.\n\nI heard it while we were checking the lanterns along Starwake Pier. It was a low note, almost musical, vibrating through the wooden platform after a gust of wind moved across the water.\n\nGreg stopped.\n\n\"Did you hear that?\"\n\nI nodded.\n\n\"Was that your guitar?\"\n\nHe held up both hands.\n\n\"Nope.\"\n\nThe stage was empty. The cables had been packed away after the Listening Fair, and the repaired railings were still marked with temporary load limits. The western section had not yet reopened.\n\nThe note sounded again.\n\nIt was deeper than anything the little stage should have produced.\n\nGreg crouched near the platform.\n\n\"That's coming from underneath.\"\n\nI thought of the second bell at Tideglass.\n\n\"Can we check safely?\"\n\nHe looked at the railing.\n\n\"Not from the closed section. Let's get someone who knows the structure.\"\n\nWe found Tamsin near the storage shed. She listened to our description, then asked us to repeat exactly where we'd stood and what the wind had been doing. She was not interested in a ghost story.\n\nDanse arrived with a vibration meter.\n\nToph came because Tamsin had asked whether the stone support beneath the pier was moving.\n\nGreg looked between them.\n\n\"I think I know what note it is.\"\n\nToph raised an eyebrow.\n\n\"That's useful, but I need to know what's making it.\"\n\n\"Fair.\"\n\nWe set up the meter on the safe platform and waited for the wind to shift.\n\nThe note came again.\n\nDanse recorded the frequency. Toph felt the slight movement through the stone support. Tamsin checked the old service map. The measurements suggested a cavity beneath the pier was resonating when water passed through a narrow opening.\n\n\"That could be connected to the old drainage route,\" I said.\n\nDanse nodded.\n\n\"Possibly. We need a water-level comparison before we say more.\"\n\nGreg tapped the edge of the stage.\n\n\"It's like a guitar body. The wood makes one part of the sound, the hollow space makes another.\"\n\nToph grinned.\n\n\"Finally, the musician says something useful.\"\n\n\"High praise.\"\n\nWe laughed.\n\nThen the wind shifted and the note grew louder.\n\nFen sent a warning from Tideglass. The tide was rising faster than the morning chart had predicted. The lower service passage was still sealed, and several surface drains were carrying more water than usual.\n\nTamsin looked at the stage.\n\n\"Close this section.\"\n\nWe moved the lanterns away from the western boards and posted a clear route to the upper path. Greg helped carry the equipment, checking with the volunteers rather than assigning jobs on the spot. Neri updated the public notice without listing any traveler's private destination.\n\nThe storm had not arrived yet.\n\nThat was why we had time.\n\nBefore sunset, the volunteer crew went over the evacuation plan one more time. A younger helper named Jo wanted to know whether we ought to warn everyone who lived near the water that the old tunnel was opening.\n\n\"It's not opening,\" Fen said from the speaking station. \"The surface drains are handling the extra flow. The lower historic passage is still sealed.\"\n\nJo looked relieved, then embarrassed. \"I told my brother there was a secret tunnel under the stage.\"\n\n\"Well,\" Greg said, \"there might be a very boring maintenance route. I'm not volunteering to write a song about the plumbing.\"\n\nI laughed, but Danse was checking the measurements with such a serious face that I stopped.\n\n\"Does the sound mean something's going to fail?\" I asked.\n\n\"We don't know yet. It might mean air is moving through a cavity. It might mean the pressure is changing. The point is to observe without treating the noise as an instruction to go inside.\"\n\nTamsin recorded the warning limits and asked Jo to help point people toward the upper stairs, where the signs had been tested at eye level for several kinds of visitors. Jo said yes. There were no new volunteers sent onto the closed platform after that.\n\nGreg pulled out his notebook. On one page he wrote the sound as a musician would hear it; on the facing page he copied the time and gauge reading from the official report. The two pages weren't interchangeable.\n\n\"Why keep them separate?\" I asked.\n\n\"So nobody ends up reading my terrible chord notation as proof the pier is safe,\" he said.\n\nDanse looked at the notebook.\n\n\"That is a surprisingly good control measure.\"\n\n\"Hey. I'm full of those.\"\n\nGreg grinned, and I saw Danse's mouth almost move into a smile.\n\nThe wind made the stage sing again. Jo looked toward the boarded walkway, then returned to their post. The discipline of staying where the crew could actually help seemed as important as any discovery.\n\nFor once, I wasn't frightened by the sound simply because we didn't understand it. I was frightened in a useful way, the kind that made us ask who was responsible for the next decision.\n\nA gust tugged at Greg's coat. He caught my hand to steady himself, then let go when the path narrowed.\n\n\"See?\" he said. \"No heroic rescue required.\"\n\n\"Not unless you plan to rescue your notebook from the rain.\"\n\nHe tucked it under his shirt and ran toward the shelter, laughing.\n\nAt dusk, the water rose beneath the pier. The low note sounded several times, each one a little shorter than the last. Danse marked the timing, and Greg wrote down the pitch changes in ordinary musical language.\n\nThey compared the notes.\n\nIt wasn't a perfect match to the second bell at Tideglass. The structures were different. But both sounds appeared when water pressure changed along the old service system.\n\n\"That doesn't make them the same instrument,\" Greg said.\n\n\"No,\" Danse agreed. \"But it gives us a testable connection.\"\n\nWe sent the records to Moonroot Archive.\n\nSeverus and Neri could compare them with the damaged ledger and the older maintenance diagrams. Fen would continue monitoring the tide. Tamsin would not reopen the western pier until the supports passed the next inspection.\n\nNobody blamed a spirit.\n\nNobody pretended there wasn't danger.\n\nAfter the work, Greg sat beside me on the safe bench and looked toward the dark water.\n\n\"You know what's weird?\" he said.\n\n\"That the pier is singing?\"\n\n\"Besides that.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"I used to think music was something you made when you wanted people to hear you. Today the sound was telling us something we needed to listen to.\"\n\n\"That's what the fair was about.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"Guess I finally learned my own lesson.\"\n\nI leaned against him.\n\nThe air smelled of rain. The first lanterns were coming on along the safe route, and the wind carried a faint note from the old wooden supports.\n\nGreg looked at me.\n\n\"Do you ever get scared that this place will stop being safe?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nHe took my hand.\n\n\"But we know how to ask for help now. That's something.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe kissed my forehead.\n\nWe sat until the rain began, then walked back to the Starwake Door without crossing the closed section.\n\nIn the morning, the water had receded. The stage no longer made the note.\n\nTamsin recorded that too.\n\nGreg went home and wrote a short melody based on the sound, careful to keep the work separate from the inspection report.\n\nHe played it for me that evening.\n\nIt was strange and beautiful.\n\n\"It doesn't need words,\" I said.\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"No. It knows what it wants to say.\"\n\nThe stage remained closed.\n\nThe music didn't have to be."},"su-50":{"number":50,"title":"The Space Between Songs","word_count":1469,"content":"The pier reopened on a day when the wind couldn't decide what it wanted.\n\nIt blew warm from the sea in the morning, cold from the ridge by noon, and sideways through the lantern hooks just as Greg arrived with his guitar. The volunteer crew had spent the previous week finishing the western boardwalk repairs and checking every railing under the load limits Tamsin had approved.\n\nEdda had tested the path.\n\nDanse had checked the fasteners.\n\nToph had listened to the stone supports beneath the timbers.\n\nFen had signed the tide schedule.\n\nThe reopening could have been a grand celebration.\n\nGreg asked for a small concert.\n\n\"Just a few songs,\" he said. \"No big announcement.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"Yeah. I want to play because I want to play.\"\n\nThat was a good enough reason.\n\nWe arranged the chairs in a half-circle beside the safe stage. The accessible path remained open. The west railing had a new maintenance marker, and the old closed sign was stored where it could be used again if future inspections required it.\n\nNothing was being declared safe forever.\n\nThe work had simply reached the point where people could use the space again.\n\nAt sunset, Greg took the stage.\n\nHe looked out at the small gathering: Mara with a basket of bread, Iroh holding two cups of tea, Toph seated near a support where she could feel the rhythm, Danse standing beside the repaired railing, and Maui helping a child hang a paper fish where the wind wouldn't tear it away.\n\nSteven was there too.\n\nHe had arrived through the Starwake Door earlier that afternoon, carrying a small bag of snacks and looking pleased to see his father doing something that belonged to him.\n\n\"Hey, Dad,\" he'd said, hugging Greg.\n\n\"Hey, buddy.\"\n\nSteven looked at the guitar.\n\n\"New song?\"\n\n\"Couple of them.\"\n\n\"Cool.\"\n\nNo interrogation. No demand that Greg perform grief or explain his relationship with Jasper. Just a son showing up because he wanted to hear his dad play.\n\nI sat near the front.\n\nGreg looked at me, grinned, and lifted the guitar.\n\n\"This first one is about a chair,\" he said.\n\nToph groaned.\n\n\"Of course it is.\"\n\nThe crowd laughed.\n\nHe played the melody he'd begun after the Listening Fair, the one with space between the phrases. The repaired guitar sounded different from before, warmer on one low note. Greg didn't hide it. He let the new sound become part of the song.\n\nThe next piece was about coming back to a place that had changed. He'd written it after building the bench at Tideglass with Maui and Ilya. It wasn't a song about being the hero of the story. It was about the little ferry bell that told people when the upper path was open.\n\nI looked toward Maui.\n\nHe smiled.\n\nThe final song was new.\n\nGreg didn't introduce it with a speech. He simply played, and the notes moved through the evening air like lantern light on water. There were spaces where I could hear the sea. There were moments when the melody almost stopped, then found another way to continue.\n\nI felt tears gather unexpectedly.\n\nNot because it was sad.\n\nBecause it sounded like the life we'd built.\n\nWhen the last note faded, the crowd applauded. Greg smiled, bowed awkwardly, and put the guitar away before anybody could ask for a song he didn't want to play.\n\nThe concert was over.\n\nThe evening wasn't.\n\nWe shared bread and tea, helped collect the chairs, and made sure the volunteers who had agreed to clean up were actually relieved when their shifts ended. Steven helped carry the snack table back to storage. Maui asked Greg about the low note in the repaired guitar, and Danse explained the bridge repair without turning it into a lecture.\n\nToph told all three men they were becoming unbearable.\n\nThey laughed.\n\nLater, when the pier was quiet, Greg and I sat on the safe bench by the railing. The lights from Lanternwell reflected across the water.\n\n\"Well?\" he asked.\n\n\"Well what?\"\n\n\"Was it worth the trouble?\"\n\nI looked at the repaired boards.\n\n\"Every argument about load limits and chair placement?\"\n\n\"Especially those.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\nI leaned against him.\n\n\"You didn't play the old song,\" I said.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Do you wish you had?\"\n\nHe shook his head.\n\n\"I played what I wanted.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nFor a while we listened to the water moving beneath the pier. The strange low note didn't sound tonight. Fen's tide chart showed ordinary conditions, and the repaired supports had passed their tests.\n\nThat didn't mean the old drainage mystery was over.\n\nIt meant we'd learned how to keep the pier safe while the larger investigation continued.\n\nThe next afternoon, Nola came to check the storage shed before the public key policy was finalized. The building belonged to the community, and Greg couldn't simply hand out permanent access because he was fond of somebody.\n\n\"There's a visitor-use key,\" she explained. \"People can borrow it for the quiet room and return it to the desk. The instrument cupboard stays locked unless a staff member opens it.\"\n\nGreg looked sheepish. \"I was going to give Jasper a key.\"\n\n\"You can give Jasper an invitation,\" Nola said. \"That might be enough.\"\n\nI appreciated that she didn't say it to embarrass him. She was protecting the instruments other musicians had lent, the tools workers needed, and the room anyone could request without having to know Greg personally.\n\n\"Could I still come sit by the water?\" I asked.\n\n\"Of course. There's a sign-up book for the room, not a membership test. You can choose whether to give a contact number for emergency closures. We don't publish who's there.\"\n\nGreg glanced at me. \"Would you like to use the visitor key?\"\n\n\"I would.\"\n\nHe looked pleased enough that I wanted to kiss him. I held off until Nola went back to the desk, then asked whether he minded.\n\n\"Mind? I have been practicing my casual romantic expression all afternoon.\"\n\nIt was a terrible expression. I laughed and kissed him anyway.\n\nFor a moment, the music and the public work fell away. His fingers rested lightly against my cheek; I could smell salt on his jacket and the faint sweetness of the lemonade we'd shared at the concert. Then he stepped back, careful not to make the kiss an answer to a question I hadn't asked.\n\n\"I like that you can come here even when I don't,\" he said.\n\n\"Me too. I have other people to visit, and other things I want to do. But I want this to be a place I return to.\"\n\nHe nodded. \"That sounds like home.\"\n\n\"One kind of home.\"\n\n\"Yeah. One kind.\"\n\nI thought about all the versions of belonging we'd argued over: a stage, a van, a small town, a valley where visitors could leave. None of them required one person to own the door. The chance to return mattered because the chance to go was real.\n\nWe took the shed's posted access rules back to Nola and asked her to confirm the dates for the next sound check. The concert was over. The responsibility to keep the place useful wasn't.\n\nGreg turned toward me.\n\n\"Do you want a key?\" he asked.\n\nI blinked.\n\n\"To what?\"\n\n\"The storage shed. Not because you have to help. Just so you can come here when you want to sit by the water.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"Yeah. You've got a life in a lot of places. I like that this can be one of them.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Then yes.\"\n\nHe placed the little key in my hand.\n\nIt was ordinary brass, with a paper tag showing the storage shed's number and the rules for borrowing equipment.\n\nNo destiny.\n\nNo ownership.\n\nJust trust, with instructions attached.\n\nI laughed.\n\n\"Very romantic.\"\n\n\"I try.\"\n\nHe kissed me, gentle and warm, then rested his forehead against mine.\n\n\"Love you,\" he whispered.\n\n\"Love you too.\"\n\nWe stayed until the last lantern was turned down.\n\nThe next morning, the pier would need sweeping. The repair fund would need another entry. Greg would have a song to finish, and I would have appointments in other districts, with other people I loved.\n\nNone of that made tonight smaller.\n\nWhen we stood to leave, Greg looked back at the empty stage.\n\n\"Should we leave the last chair out?\"\n\n\"For whom?\"\n\n\"Whoever comes next.\"\n\nI considered the clear walkway, the weather forecast, and the storage rules.\n\n\"Inside by the door,\" I said. \"Available, but not blocking the path.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\n\"Practical romance.\"\n\n\"The best kind.\"\n\nWe carried the chair together.\n\nAt the Starwake Door, he paused and offered his hand.\n\nI took it.\n\nThe space between songs was never empty.\n\nIt was where we chose to listen."}}};let count=0;for(const s of lib.series){const edits=revisions[s.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of s.chapters){const e=edits[ch.id];if(!e||ch.chapter_number!==e.number)continue;ch.content=e.content;ch.word_count=e.word_count;ch.title=e.title;count++;}}window.LANTERNWEALD_PASS12_B=count;})();
